1
00:00:00,330 --> 00:00:02,238
Richard Glossip ate his last meal.

2
00:00:02,258 --> 00:00:03,845
Then he ate another one.

3
00:00:03,865 --> 00:00:06,094
Then another. According to the Associated Press,

4
00:00:06,114 --> 00:00:08,986
he was served what was supposed to be his

5
00:00:09,006 --> 00:00:10,914
final meal at least three times.

6
00:00:11,634 --> 00:00:14,863
Over the years, Oklahoma set nine dates to execute

7
00:00:14,883 --> 00:00:16,667
him. Every single one passed.

8
00:00:16,687 --> 00:00:18,472
Some were stopped weeks before.

9
00:00:18,492 --> 00:00:20,999
One was stopped about an hour before.

10
00:00:22,319 --> 00:00:24,329
Last week, on November twelfth,

11
00:00:24,349 --> 00:00:27,983
twenty twenty six, Glossip stood in an Oklahoma courtroom,

12
00:00:28,003 --> 00:00:30,419
and pleaded guilty. Not to murder.

13
00:00:30,439 --> 00:00:32,042
To a lesser charge.

14
00:00:32,762 --> 00:00:35,642
In December, a judge will decide whether he walks

15
00:00:35,662 --> 00:00:38,219
free, or goes back to prison for decades.

16
00:00:38,239 --> 00:00:41,118
To understand how a man ends up with nine

17
00:00:41,138 --> 00:00:42,729
execution dates and no execution,

18
00:00:42,749 --> 00:00:45,628
you have to go back to a motel in

19
00:00:45,648 --> 00:00:47,883
Oklahoma City, in January nineteen ninety seven.

20
00:00:49,623 --> 00:00:51,439
Chapter 1. The motel.

21
00:00:53,539 --> 00:00:56,574
The Best Budget Inn was a cheap motel in

22
00:00:56,594 --> 00:00:59,629
Oklahoma City. It was owned by a businessman named

23
00:00:59,649 --> 00:01:02,345
Barry Van Treese. Richard Glossip was its manager.

24
00:01:02,365 --> 00:01:05,400
A nineteen year old named Justin Sneed did maintenance.

25
00:01:06,120 --> 00:01:08,191
On January seventh, nineteen ninety seven,

26
00:01:08,211 --> 00:01:11,326
Barry Van Treese was beaten to death in one

27
00:01:11,346 --> 00:01:12,720
of the motel's rooms.

28
00:01:12,740 --> 00:01:15,507
The man who killed him was Justin Sneed.

29
00:01:15,527 --> 00:01:17,598
That has never been in dispute.

30
00:01:17,618 --> 00:01:18,295
Sneed confessed.

31
00:01:19,015 --> 00:01:20,943
But Sneed told police something else.

32
00:01:20,963 --> 00:01:23,865
He said Richard Glossip had told him to do

33
00:01:23,885 --> 00:01:26,137
it. That Glossip had promised him money.

34
00:01:26,857 --> 00:01:28,288
Sneed made a deal.

35
00:01:28,308 --> 00:01:30,464
In exchange for testifying against Glossip,

36
00:01:30,484 --> 00:01:32,277
he avoided the death penalty,

37
00:01:32,297 --> 00:01:34,815
and received life in prison without parole.

38
00:01:35,535 --> 00:01:37,852
Glossip has always said he did not plan the

39
00:01:37,872 --> 00:01:40,189
killing, and did not ask anyone to commit it.

40
00:01:40,209 --> 00:01:42,525
He has admitted that after he learned what Sneed

41
00:01:42,545 --> 00:01:44,862
had done, he did not tell police right away.

42
00:01:45,582 --> 00:01:47,892
And that is the heart of this case.

43
00:01:47,912 --> 00:01:50,223
The man who swung the weapon got life.

44
00:01:50,243 --> 00:01:51,970
The man who, according to prosecutors,

45
00:01:51,990 --> 00:01:53,135
ordered it, got death.

46
00:01:53,155 --> 00:01:55,757
And the main evidence against him was the word

47
00:01:55,777 --> 00:01:56,631
of the killer.

48
00:01:58,371 --> 00:02:00,302
Chapter 2. Two trials.

49
00:02:02,402 --> 00:02:03,677
In nineteen ninety eight,

50
00:02:03,697 --> 00:02:06,267
a jury convicted Glossip of first degree murder,

51
00:02:06,287 --> 00:02:07,886
and sentenced him to death.

52
00:02:08,606 --> 00:02:09,886
In two thousand one,

53
00:02:09,906 --> 00:02:12,487
Oklahoma's highest criminal court threw the conviction out.

54
00:02:12,507 --> 00:02:15,088
It called the case against him extremely weak,

55
00:02:15,108 --> 00:02:17,364
and found his lawyer had failed him.

56
00:02:18,084 --> 00:02:19,706
In two thousand four,

57
00:02:19,726 --> 00:02:21,347
he was tried again.

58
00:02:21,367 --> 00:02:22,989
Again, Sneed testified. Again,

59
00:02:23,009 --> 00:02:25,041
the jury convicted. And again,

60
00:02:25,061 --> 00:02:26,683
the sentence was death.

61
00:02:27,403 --> 00:02:29,258
This time, the conviction stood.

62
00:02:29,278 --> 00:02:30,757
In two thousand seven,

63
00:02:30,777 --> 00:02:32,632
the appeals court upheld it,

64
00:02:32,652 --> 00:02:34,132
by a narrow margin.

65
00:02:34,152 --> 00:02:35,257
Two judges dissented.

66
00:02:35,977 --> 00:02:39,014
Glossip went to the death row unit at the

67
00:02:39,034 --> 00:02:40,712
Oklahoma State Penitentiary in McAlester.

68
00:02:40,732 --> 00:02:43,769
He would stay on death row for about twenty

69
00:02:43,789 --> 00:02:44,109
years.

70
00:02:45,049 --> 00:02:46,888
Chapter 3. The drug.

71
00:02:48,988 --> 00:02:50,369
For years, nothing happened.

72
00:02:50,389 --> 00:02:53,521
Then Oklahoma ran into a problem that was not

73
00:02:53,541 --> 00:02:54,922
about Glossip at all.

74
00:02:54,942 --> 00:02:55,623
The drugs.

75
00:02:56,343 --> 00:03:00,069
Like other states, Oklahoma was struggling to obtain lethal

76
00:03:00,089 --> 00:03:02,150
injection drugs. In twenty fourteen,

77
00:03:02,170 --> 00:03:05,064
it began using a sedative called midazolam.

78
00:03:05,084 --> 00:03:07,978
That April, the execution of another man,

79
00:03:07,998 --> 00:03:10,059
Clayton Lockett, went badly wrong.

80
00:03:10,079 --> 00:03:12,140
Lockett writhed on the gurney,

81
00:03:12,160 --> 00:03:15,054
and died after the procedure was halted.

82
00:03:15,774 --> 00:03:17,263
In October twenty fourteen,

83
00:03:17,283 --> 00:03:19,150
the state delayed several executions,

84
00:03:19,170 --> 00:03:22,546
including Glossip's, saying it did not have enough of

85
00:03:22,566 --> 00:03:23,300
the drugs.

86
00:03:24,020 --> 00:03:25,754
In January twenty fifteen,

87
00:03:25,774 --> 00:03:28,385
Oklahoma executed Charles Warner using midazolam.

88
00:03:28,405 --> 00:03:32,332
Glossip and other prisoners challenged the drug in court.

89
00:03:32,352 --> 00:03:36,278
And the U.S. Supreme Court agreed to hear the

90
00:03:36,298 --> 00:03:40,225
case. It stopped Glossip's January execution while it did.

91
00:03:40,945 --> 00:03:44,158
The case became known as Glossip versus Gross.

92
00:03:44,178 --> 00:03:45,774
On June twenty ninth,

93
00:03:45,794 --> 00:03:47,795
twenty fifteen, the court ruled,

94
00:03:47,815 --> 00:03:51,432
five to four, that the prisoners had not proven

95
00:03:51,452 --> 00:03:53,857
midazolam was unconstitutional. Justice Stephen Breyer,

96
00:03:53,877 --> 00:03:55,473
in dissent, went further.

97
00:03:55,493 --> 00:03:59,110
He questioned whether the death penalty itself was constitutional.

98
00:03:59,830 --> 00:04:02,929
Justice Sonia Sotomayor wrote a dissent of her own.

99
00:04:02,949 --> 00:04:06,048
If midazolam did not work as the state claimed,

100
00:04:06,068 --> 00:04:09,167
she wrote, being executed with it could be the

101
00:04:09,187 --> 00:04:11,940
chemical equivalent of being burned at the stake.

102
00:04:11,960 --> 00:04:15,059
She would later write the opinion that changed Glossip's

103
00:04:15,079 --> 00:04:15,405
life.

104
00:04:16,125 --> 00:04:20,102
Glossip had lost. And Oklahoma set a new date.

105
00:04:20,122 --> 00:04:21,878
September sixteenth, twenty fifteen.

106
00:04:23,418 --> 00:04:25,594
Chapter 4. The last meals.

107
00:04:27,694 --> 00:04:30,503
A last meal is one of the final rituals

108
00:04:30,523 --> 00:04:33,333
before an execution. The prisoner chooses what to eat,

109
00:04:33,353 --> 00:04:34,591
within the prison's limits.

110
00:04:34,611 --> 00:04:36,163
Then the clock keeps running.

111
00:04:36,883 --> 00:04:38,517
For a prisoner in Oklahoma,

112
00:04:38,537 --> 00:04:40,502
the final days have a routine.

113
00:04:40,522 --> 00:04:43,480
According to Vice, the condemned man is moved cell

114
00:04:43,500 --> 00:04:45,795
by cell closer to the execution chamber,

115
00:04:45,815 --> 00:04:47,119
watched around the clock,

116
00:04:47,139 --> 00:04:50,096
and allowed to say goodbye to the people he

117
00:04:50,116 --> 00:04:53,073
loves. Glossip went through that routine more than once.

118
00:04:53,793 --> 00:04:55,682
According to an AP timeline,

119
00:04:55,702 --> 00:04:59,117
on September fifteenth, the day before his scheduled execution,

120
00:04:59,137 --> 00:05:02,553
Glossip was served what was supposed to be his

121
00:05:02,573 --> 00:05:05,988
last meal. Chicken fried steak with mashed potatoes and

122
00:05:06,008 --> 00:05:08,278
a dinner roll. Fish and chips.

123
00:05:08,298 --> 00:05:10,950
A bacon cheeseburger. And a strawberry malt.

124
00:05:11,670 --> 00:05:14,512
The next day, a few hours before the execution,

125
00:05:14,532 --> 00:05:17,375
Oklahoma's Court of Criminal Appeals granted a two week

126
00:05:17,395 --> 00:05:19,919
reprieve, to review new claims about his case.

127
00:05:19,939 --> 00:05:22,781
According to Vice, the stay came about three hours

128
00:05:22,801 --> 00:05:24,689
before he was due to die.

129
00:05:25,409 --> 00:05:27,442
The new date was September thirtieth.

130
00:05:27,462 --> 00:05:28,810
On September twenty eighth,

131
00:05:28,830 --> 00:05:31,889
the appeals court voted three to two to let

132
00:05:31,909 --> 00:05:33,942
it go ahead. On September thirtieth,

133
00:05:33,962 --> 00:05:36,678
the U.S. Supreme Court refused to stop it.

134
00:05:37,398 --> 00:05:38,842
One justice, Stephen Breyer,

135
00:05:38,862 --> 00:05:40,671
would have granted a stay.

136
00:05:40,691 --> 00:05:41,768
He was outvoted.

137
00:05:42,488 --> 00:05:43,937
And so, once again,

138
00:05:43,957 --> 00:05:46,139
Glossip was served a last meal.

139
00:05:46,159 --> 00:05:47,975
According to the AP timeline,

140
00:05:47,995 --> 00:05:50,177
this one included a medium pizza,

141
00:05:50,197 --> 00:05:52,380
two orders of fish and chips,

142
00:05:52,400 --> 00:05:54,949
a bacon cheeseburger, and another strawberry malt.

143
00:05:54,969 --> 00:05:58,253
Media outlets have reported versions of his order over

144
00:05:58,273 --> 00:06:01,557
the years. The strawberry shake appears in almost all

145
00:06:01,577 --> 00:06:02,291
of them.

146
00:06:03,011 --> 00:06:05,650
According to reports, he was on the phone with

147
00:06:05,670 --> 00:06:06,832
a British TV channel,

148
00:06:06,852 --> 00:06:08,900
Sky News, as the meal was served.

149
00:06:09,620 --> 00:06:12,258
Then, about an hour before the scheduled time,

150
00:06:12,278 --> 00:06:14,252
Governor Mary Fallin issued a stay.

151
00:06:14,272 --> 00:06:15,913
Not because of new evidence.

152
00:06:15,933 --> 00:06:17,242
Because of the drugs.

153
00:06:18,982 --> 00:06:20,984
Chapter 5. The wrong drug.

154
00:06:23,084 --> 00:06:25,412
The state's protocol called for three drugs.

155
00:06:25,432 --> 00:06:28,430
The last one was supposed to be potassium chloride,

156
00:06:28,450 --> 00:06:29,772
which stops the heart.

157
00:06:29,792 --> 00:06:32,791
What had been delivered to the prison was a

158
00:06:32,811 --> 00:06:34,132
different drug. Potassium acetate.

159
00:06:34,852 --> 00:06:37,302
Officials had noticed in the final hours.

160
00:06:37,322 --> 00:06:39,419
And the governor stopped the execution.

161
00:06:40,139 --> 00:06:42,371
Then came a worse discovery.

162
00:06:42,391 --> 00:06:44,173
In October twenty fifteen,

163
00:06:44,193 --> 00:06:46,425
reporting revealed that Charles Warner,

164
00:06:46,445 --> 00:06:50,478
executed in January, had been given potassium acetate too.

165
00:06:50,498 --> 00:06:53,181
The prison's logs said potassium chloride.

166
00:06:53,201 --> 00:06:56,784
His autopsy report recorded vials of potassium acetate.

167
00:06:57,504 --> 00:06:58,970
A grand jury investigated.

168
00:06:58,990 --> 00:07:01,200
Its report, released in twenty sixteen,

169
00:07:01,220 --> 00:07:03,801
confirmed the wrong drug had been used,

170
00:07:03,821 --> 00:07:05,659
and criticized the people responsible.

171
00:07:06,379 --> 00:07:08,432
On October second, twenty fifteen,

172
00:07:08,452 --> 00:07:12,163
Oklahoma's appeals court granted the attorney general's request to

173
00:07:12,183 --> 00:07:14,236
put all executions on hold.

174
00:07:14,256 --> 00:07:16,310
Glossip's next date, in November,

175
00:07:16,330 --> 00:07:18,383
came and went. For Oklahoma,

176
00:07:18,403 --> 00:07:20,456
the pause would last years.

177
00:07:21,176 --> 00:07:24,096
Glossip had now come within hours of execution twice.

178
00:07:24,116 --> 00:07:26,383
And he had eaten his last meal,

179
00:07:26,403 --> 00:07:28,344
by most accounts, at least twice.

180
00:07:29,664 --> 00:07:32,628
By then, his case had become famous.

181
00:07:32,648 --> 00:07:36,464
The nun and anti death penalty activist Sister Helen

182
00:07:36,484 --> 00:07:39,022
Prejean, author of Dead Man Walking,

183
00:07:39,042 --> 00:07:40,727
became his spiritual adviser.

184
00:07:40,747 --> 00:07:43,711
The actress Susan Sarandon campaigned for him.

185
00:07:43,731 --> 00:07:47,121
In twenty seventeen, a four part documentary series,

186
00:07:47,141 --> 00:07:50,105
Killing Richard Glossip, aired on Investigation Discovery.

187
00:07:51,045 --> 00:07:53,336
Chapter 6. The lawmakers.

188
00:07:55,436 --> 00:07:56,844
When Oklahoma restarted executions,

189
00:07:56,864 --> 00:07:58,629
it came back for Glossip.

190
00:07:58,649 --> 00:08:00,414
In July twenty twenty two,

191
00:08:00,434 --> 00:08:02,556
he was given a new date.

192
00:08:02,576 --> 00:08:03,627
September twenty second.

193
00:08:04,347 --> 00:08:07,595
But this time, some of the pressure came from

194
00:08:07,615 --> 00:08:10,499
an unexpected direction. In early twenty twenty two,

195
00:08:10,519 --> 00:08:13,403
a bipartisan group of thirty five Oklahoma lawmakers,

196
00:08:13,423 --> 00:08:15,581
led by Republican representative Kevin McDugle,

197
00:08:15,601 --> 00:08:17,759
a supporter of the death penalty,

198
00:08:17,779 --> 00:08:19,211
asked a law firm,

199
00:08:19,231 --> 00:08:22,115
Reed Smith, to review the case for free.

200
00:08:22,835 --> 00:08:24,430
In June twenty twenty two,

201
00:08:24,450 --> 00:08:27,336
the firm released a report of more than three

202
00:08:27,356 --> 00:08:28,950
hundred pages. Among its findings,

203
00:08:28,970 --> 00:08:31,856
it said the district attorney's office had told police

204
00:08:31,876 --> 00:08:34,762
to destroy a box of evidence before the second

205
00:08:34,782 --> 00:08:37,669
trial. The current district attorney disputed that there was

206
00:08:37,689 --> 00:08:39,606
direct evidence of such an order.

207
00:08:40,326 --> 00:08:43,000
The report also said a videotape from the night

208
00:08:43,020 --> 00:08:44,796
of the murder had been lost.

209
00:08:44,816 --> 00:08:46,892
And it concluded that no reasonable jury,

210
00:08:46,912 --> 00:08:48,089
hearing the full story,

211
00:08:48,109 --> 00:08:49,886
would have convicted Glossip of murder.

212
00:08:50,606 --> 00:08:53,206
The report said that several jurors from the trial,

213
00:08:53,226 --> 00:08:55,826
shown a police interrogation video the jury had never

214
00:08:55,846 --> 00:08:58,446
seen, said they would not have voted to convict.

215
00:08:59,166 --> 00:09:00,484
McDugle put it bluntly.

216
00:09:00,504 --> 00:09:01,822
The report, he said,

217
00:09:01,842 --> 00:09:04,833
confirmed in his mind that Oklahoma had an innocent

218
00:09:04,853 --> 00:09:06,171
man on death row.

219
00:09:07,491 --> 00:09:10,215
Governor Kevin Stitt granted a sixty day stay.

220
00:09:10,235 --> 00:09:12,274
The date moved to December eighth.

221
00:09:12,294 --> 00:09:13,646
Stitt granted another stay.

222
00:09:13,666 --> 00:09:16,391
The date moved to February twenty twenty three.

223
00:09:16,411 --> 00:09:17,763
Then to May eighteenth.

224
00:09:18,703 --> 00:09:21,714
Chapter 7. The prosecutor changes sides.

225
00:09:23,814 --> 00:09:25,718
In January twenty twenty three,

226
00:09:25,738 --> 00:09:28,027
Oklahoma got a new attorney general,

227
00:09:28,047 --> 00:09:31,490
Gentner Drummond. He appointed an independent counsel to review

228
00:09:31,510 --> 00:09:32,259
the case.

229
00:09:32,979 --> 00:09:34,673
In early twenty twenty three,

230
00:09:34,693 --> 00:09:37,757
the state disclosed notes that had not been shared

231
00:09:37,777 --> 00:09:40,841
with the defense. They suggested Sneed had told prosecutors

232
00:09:40,861 --> 00:09:43,925
he was being treated by a psychiatrist and prescribed

233
00:09:43,945 --> 00:09:47,009
lithium. At trial, Sneed had testified that he had

234
00:09:47,029 --> 00:09:48,380
never seen a psychiatrist.

235
00:09:49,100 --> 00:09:51,163
Drummond did not say Glossip was innocent.

236
00:09:51,183 --> 00:09:53,840
But he said the conviction could no longer stand.

237
00:09:53,860 --> 00:09:56,220
He asked the courts to throw it out.

238
00:09:56,240 --> 00:09:58,898
An attorney general, asking to vacate a death sentence

239
00:09:58,918 --> 00:10:00,683
that his own office had won.

240
00:10:01,403 --> 00:10:03,842
On April twentieth, twenty twenty three,

241
00:10:03,862 --> 00:10:06,711
the Oklahoma Court of Criminal Appeals refused.

242
00:10:06,731 --> 00:10:08,350
The conviction would stand.

243
00:10:09,070 --> 00:10:12,202
Days later, Glossip went before the state's pardon and

244
00:10:12,222 --> 00:10:14,653
parole board. One member had stepped aside,

245
00:10:14,673 --> 00:10:16,754
because his wife had prosecuted Glossip.

246
00:10:16,774 --> 00:10:18,855
The vote was two to two.

247
00:10:18,875 --> 00:10:20,606
A tie meant no clemency.

248
00:10:21,326 --> 00:10:23,814
Every road in Oklahoma was now closed.

249
00:10:23,834 --> 00:10:26,323
The execution was set for May eighteenth.

250
00:10:27,643 --> 00:10:29,224
On May fifth, the U.S.

251
00:10:29,244 --> 00:10:30,505
Supreme Court stepped in,

252
00:10:30,525 --> 00:10:33,387
and stayed the execution while it considered the case.

253
00:10:33,407 --> 00:10:34,668
By the AP's count,

254
00:10:34,688 --> 00:10:37,229
it was the ninth date set for him.

255
00:10:37,249 --> 00:10:38,831
It would be the last.

256
00:10:40,571 --> 00:10:42,665
Chapter 8. The Supreme Court.

257
00:10:44,765 --> 00:10:46,984
On October ninth, twenty twenty four,

258
00:10:47,004 --> 00:10:49,223
the case was argued in Washington.

259
00:10:49,243 --> 00:10:51,835
Unusually, Oklahoma's attorney general stood with Glossip,

260
00:10:51,855 --> 00:10:54,820
asking the court to order a new trial.

261
00:10:54,840 --> 00:10:58,179
The court appointed an outside lawyer to defend the

262
00:10:58,199 --> 00:10:58,552
conviction.

263
00:10:59,272 --> 00:11:00,907
On February twenty fifth,

264
00:11:00,927 --> 00:11:03,390
twenty twenty five, the court ruled.

265
00:11:03,410 --> 00:11:05,046
Writing for the majority,

266
00:11:05,066 --> 00:11:08,770
Justice Sonia Sotomayor found that the prosecution had violated

267
00:11:08,790 --> 00:11:11,667
its constitutional duty to correct false testimony.

268
00:11:11,687 --> 00:11:14,150
Sneed's testimony about his psychiatric treatment.

269
00:11:14,870 --> 00:11:18,013
The rule goes back to a nineteen fifty nine

270
00:11:18,033 --> 00:11:19,419
case, Napue versus Illinois.

271
00:11:19,439 --> 00:11:22,582
If prosecutors know their own witness is giving false

272
00:11:22,602 --> 00:11:24,339
testimony, they must correct it.

273
00:11:24,359 --> 00:11:25,745
Here, the court found,

274
00:11:25,765 --> 00:11:26,800
they had not.

275
00:11:27,520 --> 00:11:29,323
Because Sneed was the key witness,

276
00:11:29,343 --> 00:11:32,057
the court reasoned, the jury needed to know he

277
00:11:32,077 --> 00:11:33,576
had not told the truth.

278
00:11:33,596 --> 00:11:35,095
The conviction was thrown out.

279
00:11:35,115 --> 00:11:36,918
Glossip would get a new trial.

280
00:11:37,638 --> 00:11:40,064
Justices Clarence Thomas and Samuel Alito dissented.

281
00:11:40,084 --> 00:11:42,510
Justice Neil Gorsuch did not take part.

282
00:11:42,530 --> 00:11:45,655
For the first time in more than twenty years,

283
00:11:45,675 --> 00:11:48,800
Richard Glossip was no longer under a sentence of

284
00:11:48,820 --> 00:11:49,149
death.

285
00:11:50,689 --> 00:11:52,459
Chapter 9. Outside.

286
00:11:54,559 --> 00:11:56,024
But he was not free.

287
00:11:56,044 --> 00:11:57,509
In June twenty twenty five,

288
00:11:57,529 --> 00:12:00,182
the state announced it would try him again for

289
00:12:00,202 --> 00:12:01,964
murder, without seeking the death penalty.

290
00:12:01,984 --> 00:12:04,340
He was moved to the Oklahoma County jail.

291
00:12:05,060 --> 00:12:07,198
In July, a judge denied him bond.

292
00:12:07,218 --> 00:12:09,973
Then the case went through a series of judges,

293
00:12:09,993 --> 00:12:11,207
as several stepped aside.

294
00:12:11,927 --> 00:12:13,969
On May fourteenth, twenty twenty six,

295
00:12:13,989 --> 00:12:15,344
a judge granted bond.

296
00:12:15,364 --> 00:12:16,719
Five hundred thousand dollars,

297
00:12:16,739 --> 00:12:18,438
with conditions. A GPS monitor.

298
00:12:18,458 --> 00:12:21,531
A curfew from ten at night to seven in

299
00:12:21,551 --> 00:12:24,625
the morning. He had to live with his wife.

300
00:12:25,345 --> 00:12:28,072
Richard Glossip walked out of custody for the first

301
00:12:28,092 --> 00:12:29,597
time in nearly thirty years.

302
00:12:31,117 --> 00:12:33,001
He was sixty three years old.

303
00:12:33,021 --> 00:12:34,270
In twenty twenty two,

304
00:12:34,290 --> 00:12:35,857
while still on death row,

305
00:12:35,877 --> 00:12:37,444
he had married Lea Rodger,

306
00:12:37,464 --> 00:12:39,030
an anti death penalty advocate.

307
00:12:39,050 --> 00:12:40,617
Now, for the first time,

308
00:12:40,637 --> 00:12:42,838
they could live under the same roof.

309
00:12:43,558 --> 00:12:45,003
A retrial was set.

310
00:12:45,023 --> 00:12:48,300
His lawyers won permission for new DNA testing on

311
00:12:48,320 --> 00:12:50,131
swabs from the crime scene.

312
00:12:50,151 --> 00:12:52,329
Jury selection was scheduled for December.

313
00:12:53,269 --> 00:12:54,725
Chapter 10. The plea.

314
00:12:56,825 --> 00:12:58,541
It never got that far.

315
00:12:58,561 --> 00:13:00,625
On November twelfth, twenty twenty six,

316
00:13:00,645 --> 00:13:03,750
Glossip pleaded guilty to being an accessory after the

317
00:13:03,770 --> 00:13:05,486
fact to first degree murder.

318
00:13:05,506 --> 00:13:07,222
The murder charge was dropped.

319
00:13:07,942 --> 00:13:09,233
His lawyer, Don Knight,

320
00:13:09,253 --> 00:13:12,184
said the plea acknowledges that Glossip helped put plexiglass

321
00:13:12,204 --> 00:13:14,479
over a broken window at the motel.

322
00:13:14,499 --> 00:13:17,430
According to Knight, Van Treese's body was inside the

323
00:13:17,450 --> 00:13:20,381
room, but Glossip did not know it was there,

324
00:13:20,401 --> 00:13:21,693
and never saw it.

325
00:13:22,413 --> 00:13:24,686
The attorney general saw it differently.

326
00:13:24,706 --> 00:13:28,126
Today, Drummond said, Richard Glossip finally took some measure

327
00:13:28,146 --> 00:13:31,566
of responsibility for the murder of Barry Van Treese.

328
00:13:32,286 --> 00:13:35,363
The maximum sentence for the charge is forty five

329
00:13:35,383 --> 00:13:38,459
years. The attorney general and the Van Treese family

330
00:13:38,479 --> 00:13:40,524
are asking for all of it.

331
00:13:40,544 --> 00:13:43,621
Glossip's lawyers have argued that time he has already

332
00:13:43,641 --> 00:13:44,997
served could mean release.

333
00:13:45,017 --> 00:13:47,061
The decision belongs to the judge.

334
00:13:47,081 --> 00:13:49,814
Sentencing is set for December seventeenth and eighteenth.

335
00:13:50,534 --> 00:13:52,170
And through all of it,

336
00:13:52,190 --> 00:13:53,495
there is a family.

337
00:13:53,515 --> 00:13:56,476
Barry Van Treese was killed in nineteen ninety seven.

338
00:13:56,496 --> 00:13:59,457
His family has lived with this case for almost

339
00:13:59,477 --> 00:14:02,438
thirty years. They have criticized the attorney general's handling

340
00:14:02,458 --> 00:14:04,756
of the case. Whatever happens in December,

341
00:14:04,776 --> 00:14:07,737
his death is the reason any of this exists.

342
00:14:09,257 --> 00:14:12,586
Nine execution dates. At least three last meals.

343
00:14:12,606 --> 00:14:14,679
Two convictions overturned. And now,

344
00:14:14,699 --> 00:14:17,609
a guilty plea to a lesser crime,

345
00:14:17,629 --> 00:14:19,284
after nearly thirty years.

346
00:14:20,004 --> 00:14:21,586
So what do you think?

347
00:14:21,606 --> 00:14:24,471
Justice finally done, or justice delayed for too long?

348
00:14:24,491 --> 00:14:26,073
Tell us in the comments.

349
00:14:26,093 --> 00:14:28,957
And to see what became of California's most famous

350
00:14:28,977 --> 00:14:31,842
death row prison, watch our story about the San

351
00:14:31,862 --> 00:14:33,444
Quentin marathon, on this channel.
